  "body": "The last item left open by the reasoning arc.\n\n## The gap\n\nModel metadata — vision support, context length, prices — was matched against **every provider's models flattened into one namespace**, first writer wins, and the winner was decided by whichever order models.dev happened to serialise its JSON.\n\nMeasured against the live 2026-08 index (193 providers):\n\n```\nmodel ids claimed by >1 provider     3,757\n  ...disagreeing about vision          234\n```\n\nSo a model could be told it was blind because an unrelated provider hosts something with the same name — and vision routing then diverts a request that never needed diverting.\n\n## What changed\n\n**1. The model's own provider is consulted first**, via the same alias map the reasoning-capability lookup already uses (`open_router` → `openrouter`, `fireworks` → `fireworks-ai`). The cross-provider index stays as a *fallback*, because most of what a gateway resells is not published under that gateway.\n\n**2. The fallback index accumulates field by field** instead of first-writer-wins, so whether a field is populated at all no longer depends on upstream serialisation order.\n\n## The correction that mattered\n\nBoth are **merges, not replacements**. My first version chose the scoped match wholesale — it looked more correct and **cost 11 models metadata they have today**, which I only found by running it against the real index rather than a fixture. That's a regression however much more authoritative the source is.\n\nSo per field: the model's own provider wins where it says anything, and a namesake elsewhere fills only the gaps.\n\n## Verification\n\nAgainst the real 4.2 MB index:\n\n```\nmodels whose metadata changes        1,936\nmodels that LOSE metadata                0\n```\n\nGuards mutation-tested, each failing **by name**:\n\n| mutation | caught by |\n| --- | --- |\n| M1 scoping removed | `models_own_provider_decides_what_it_can_do` |\n| M2 scoped chosen wholesale | `sparse_provider_entry_does_not_strip` |\n| M3 alias map ignored | `provider_alias_map_is_used_for_scoping_too` |\n| M4 flat index back to first-wins | `sparse_provider_entry_does_not_strip` |\n\n**M3 survived a first pass** — the test asserted an answer the flat fallback happened to give anyway. It now uses a competing provider that answers differently *and* is reached first, so the alias is the only thing that can produce the expected result.\n\nLocal CI: `ruff format --check`, `ruff check`, `ty` clean; pytest **4,840 passed** with the documented 6 `test_install_ps1_*` baseline failures.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **1,936 models change what MCC believes about them.** That is the point, but it is a large behavioural surface: some models will newly be considered vision-capable (or not), which changes when the vision adapter fires. The direction is toward the provider's own answer.\n- **Two providers that both describe a model and disagree still resolve by iteration order** in the fallback — which now only matters when the model's own provider says nothing about that field. Making it deterministic needs a rule for which provider to believe, and there is no evidence yet for what that rule should be.\n- Enrichment still only fills fields the provider itself left null, unchanged.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
